 routine i wouldn't sleep at  all and then i'd switch the routine to  like this other routine so like in  another phase i switched to walking on  the treadmill but again if anything that  routine i wouldn't sleep so basically it  meant that i was trapped and my husband  was also trapped because we couldn't go  out with friends like i don't even want  him to go out alone because if he wasn't  home that would throw me off it just  felt like things were not in its place  yeah it was bad  as my therapist put it she says  everything had to be in its place for me  to sleep and that's not real life that  didn't and like it doesn't allow for me  to  live  normal life so i realized i was  basically just doing what i did with my  eating disorder but i was doing it with  my sleep like i was trying to manipulate  it and control it as a coping mechanism  for my anxiety in the process as a  result it was just kind of ruining and  running my life where am i at  today well i'm still doing lots of  therapy that's probably never going to  change and i'm constantly trying to kind  of purposefully [ __ ] with my routine  just enough that i don't get into that  ocd like obsession around sleep but i  also need to explore all of my options  for actually taking something to sleep  which i can do now because you know i'm  done breastfeeding and i'm done having  babies as far as i'm concerned right now  so first i just did herbals and  supplements like melatonin and  passionflower and magnesium and valerian  root and they would work sometimes like  i would say 50 of the time melatonin  would just like not be enough even at  really high doses which you know we  don't want to get into that so i was  needing to take ativan on top of that  you also don't want to be taking ativan  every night for the rest of your life  that is not recommended so for lots of  reasons i felt like that was just like  an unhealthy habit that i really need to  figure out how to break next i tried cbd  oil and i actually tried a whole bunch  of different ones some with thc some  without and they worked a bit just to  take the edge off but i think i'm also  kind of too far gone for that alone i'm  just a little too for the natural route  apparently  so i finally decided i was going to give  actual pharmaceutical medication a go  and i gotta say i resisted for so long  but it's been probably the best decision  i've ever made for myself and my health  i just feel like i resisted because i  didn't want to get like addicted to  something that i maybe couldn't take  while i was pregnant or nursing but like  i said i think it's safe to say that i'm  done and i'm past that phase of my life  so i can start to really focus just on  what's best for me so i'm currently  taking something called dave ego i'm not  a psychologist not a psychiatrist so  again disclaimer this is not a  suggestion to take any medications never  mind sleep medications but anyways it  was suggested to me by my doctor because  it's i guess unique in the sleep drug  world it works differently than other  sleep medications so apparently it may  be kind of less dependence forming but i  don't know i don't bank on that but  let's be real at least to me i feel like  at least psychologically any sleep  medication is gonna be somewhat  dependence forming but it has  definitely  changed my life it has changed my life  so i find that now i only have like one  to two nights a week where i have some  anxiety around sleep and i know i'm  having anxiety when i get up to pee like  10 to 12 times in the night i know it's  not because i actually have to pee it's  just because it's an anxious habit where  i become  almost like hyper aware of every  sensation in my body whether it's like a  little hunger or gotta pee or whatever  and i obsess over it even if i have like  a whisper of a thought that i have to  pee and then it's this obsession that  i've gotta get up i gotta go pee i gotta  go to bed oh i don't need to pee again i  gotta go and then i do that over and  over again and that's just like me and  my anxiety but i know it's working for  me because my sleep is just becoming  more intuitive and more natural and less  ritualistic so  sometimes i read before bed sometimes i  go right to bed and i just turn off the  light and sometimes i'll walk on the  treadmill watching a show and sometimes  i go to dinner and i have a drink like  it is so awesome  to be flexible i'm now able to gather  consistent data that i can sleep i can  do this my body's not broken that alone  i feel like helps to ease the anxiety  that i won't sleep the other thing that  i did that i feel like made a really big  difference that is like not medication  related is that i stopped setting an  alarm so if you remember from my 4 a.m  morning routine video i used to actually  get up before the kids would get up and  i would get like either a workout done  or i would answer emails get a little  bit of work done and that was generally  easy for me at the time because my body  was just so primed to get up like i just  had this energy to go even though i was  exhausted and i loved that quiet me time  i really did i also think i was scared  not to set an alarm not necessarily  because i would just sleep in but  because i was worried that if i didn't  then i would be kind of in and out of  that sleep always checking my phone  unsure of what time it was and then  thinking like should i get up should i  try to be productive should i try to  answer email should i start making  breakfast should i get a workout now  before the kids were like i was worried  about that keeping me up and i thought  maybe that would make me actually sleep  less and also i just want to say that  like skipping the workout wasn't a  rational concern for me i could be  laying in bed at night and i would be  able to rationally just tell myself yeah  you know what if you don't get up if you  don't have to exercise if you're really  tired doesn't matter you can sleep in  but then there was this like always  lingering in the back of my head in my  mind as i tried to go back to sleep in  the early morning that being like you  gotta be more productive you gotta do  more you gotta do more which of course  would make me less productive because i  wouldn't sleep and then i wasn't rested  and i'd kind of just like move through  the day like a zombie so i decided to  abolish  that morning routine abby you're not  gonna work out in the morning anymore  you're not gonna answer emails you're  not going to make breakfast until the  kids are up and they are begging you to  make the breakfast you're not even gonna  set an alarm you're gonna wait until the  kids wake you up because they are  calling for you and then you're just  gonna figure out the day does it mean  some days i don't get in as much  exercise or get as much work done in the  day maybe but not really i actually  think i'm actually more productive  because i'm just better rested so  basically by removing the pressure i  felt at night to get up early that alone  made a huge difference in my ability to  just relax and i know not everyone has  the luxury to be able to be flexible on  their schedule like that but i don't  know that has been just super helpful  for me but yeah when i shot my video on  my pei trip which you can watch right  here i mentioned that i feel that eating  disorders don't always just go away and  what i meant by that at least in my life  is that they're kind of just a temporary  band-aid for a greater bigger problem  and for me that problem is my anxiety  and it's really common for people with  addictions or mental health disorders  it's kind of just like swap disorders  once one coping mechanism loses its  effectiveness we just kind of like trade  it in for another and that's essentially  what i did in my life i don't think i've  fixed the actual problem but i feel like  medicating has definitely helped me at  least get to a better place physically i  just can't effectively manage my anxiety  when i'm sleep deprived and when my  anxiety is kind of at its lowest my  desire to control is at its lowest as  well so right now at least i feel like  this is what's working for me and i'm  just going to continue to try to like  use these drugs because they're working  for me while challenging myself outside  my comfort zone or like my safe routine  and i'll do that until i get to a place  where  i don't need these crutches anymore and  i think i'm actually getting  relatively close so i hope that was  enlightening for you guys or at least  just kind of like good to know that's  kind of where i came from and where i am  today if you like this video don't  forget to give it  that thumbs up chef's kiss  leave me a comment below if there are  other topics that you guys want to hear  me talk more about subscribe to this  channel and i will see you next time on  abby's kitchen  bye
 

